1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:03,308
ZERO forgot his mom's birthday.

2
00:00:03,608 --> 00:00:05,480
It's tomorrow.

3
00:00:10,530 --> 00:00:12,027
She lives on the other side

4
00:00:12,027 --> 00:00:12,858
of the country.

5
00:00:13,158 --> 00:00:15,078
So how is that even possible?

6
00:00:25,478 --> 00:00:27,648
Between this counter and her front

7
00:00:27,648 --> 00:00:29,690
door, something enormous happens

8
00:00:29,690 --> 00:00:31,286
while the country sleeps.

9
00:00:31,586 --> 00:00:33,458
A truck.

10
00:00:33,858 --> 00:00:34,818
A plane.

11
00:00:35,018 --> 00:00:37,104
A building that sorts more than four

12
00:00:37,104 --> 00:00:39,074
hundred thousand packages an hour.

13
00:00:39,274 --> 00:00:42,298
Tonight, ZERO is staying awake to see it.

14
00:00:43,448 --> 00:00:45,320
Three questions.

15
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:47,390
How does the system know where

16
00:00:47,390 --> 00:00:48,452
this box is going?

17
00:00:48,752 --> 00:00:50,192
Who pays for a plane to fly

18
00:00:50,192 --> 00:00:51,152
through the night?

19
00:00:51,452 --> 00:00:52,604
And what happens when

20
00:00:52,604 --> 00:00:53,756
something goes wrong?

21
00:00:54,056 --> 00:00:56,192
Because tonight, something will.

22
00:00:58,142 --> 00:00:59,918
To ZERO, it's a gift.

23
00:01:00,768 --> 00:01:02,712
To the system, it's this.

24
00:01:03,012 --> 00:01:04,884
A barcode.

25
00:01:05,034 --> 00:01:06,114
An address,

26
00:01:06,214 --> 00:01:07,558
a service level,

27
00:01:07,658 --> 00:01:09,530
a deadline.

28
00:01:10,430 --> 00:01:12,891
Every time the box passes a scanner,

29
00:01:12,891 --> 00:01:14,942
the system learns where it is.

30
00:01:15,842 --> 00:01:17,786
That's what tracking really is.

31
00:01:18,686 --> 00:01:21,038
Not a GPS inside your box.

32
00:01:21,938 --> 00:01:23,546
A trail of scans.

33
00:01:24,796 --> 00:01:26,941
From the counter, the box goes to a

34
00:01:26,941 --> 00:01:28,780
truck heading for the airport.

35
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:31,552
It's raining.

36
00:01:31,852 --> 00:01:33,724
The box doesn't care.

37
00:01:34,124 --> 00:01:35,996
ZERO does.

38
00:01:37,246 --> 00:01:39,334
Somebody is paying for this truck,

39
00:01:39,634 --> 00:01:41,506
this driver,

40
00:01:41,656 --> 00:01:43,528
and this fuel.

41
00:01:44,478 --> 00:01:46,350
Keep that in mind.

42
00:01:48,750 --> 00:01:50,562
At the airport, the boxes

43
00:01:50,562 --> 00:01:52,230
don't board one by one.

44
00:01:53,130 --> 00:01:55,002
They're packed into these.

45
00:01:56,530 --> 00:01:58,406
Containers shaped to fit the

46
00:01:58,406 --> 00:01:59,746
inside of the plane.

47
00:02:05,130 --> 00:02:07,722
But the plane isn't flying to mom's city.

48
00:02:08,730 --> 00:02:11,034
It's flying to Louisville, Kentucky.

49
00:02:11,934 --> 00:02:13,429
And so are planes from all

50
00:02:13,429 --> 00:02:14,406
over the country.

51
00:02:15,556 --> 00:02:17,468
Louisville is within a four-hour

52
00:02:17,468 --> 00:02:19,619
flight of ninety-five percent of the

53
00:02:19,619 --> 00:02:21,292
people in the United States.

54
00:02:22,192 --> 00:02:24,064
That's not an accident.

55
00:02:24,364 --> 00:02:26,236
That's why the hub is here.

56
00:02:27,586 --> 00:02:30,490
Late at night, planes line up in the dark.

57
00:02:31,540 --> 00:02:33,856
At the busiest moments, about

58
00:02:33,856 --> 00:02:35,692
one lands every minute.

59
00:02:37,892 --> 00:02:39,764
This is Worldport.

60
00:02:40,064 --> 00:02:42,128
UPS's main air hub.

61
00:02:42,928 --> 00:02:45,280
Five point two million square feet.

62
00:02:46,180 --> 00:02:48,532
Roughly ninety football fields,

63
00:02:48,832 --> 00:02:50,704
under one roof.

64
00:02:53,254 --> 00:02:55,630
Some planes don't even stop on the ramp.

65
00:02:56,530 --> 00:02:58,173
They pull right up to the

66
00:02:58,173 --> 00:02:59,554
building, nose first,

67
00:02:59,854 --> 00:03:01,774
like a car into a garage.

68
00:03:03,324 --> 00:03:04,980
The containers come off,

69
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:06,528
the doors open,

70
00:03:06,678 --> 00:03:08,718
and the boxes go onto belts.

71
00:03:09,468 --> 00:03:11,340
Small boxes,

72
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:13,512
big boxes,

73
00:03:13,712 --> 00:03:15,473
and weird-shaped ones each

74
00:03:15,473 --> 00:03:16,760
get their own lane.

75
00:03:19,110 --> 00:03:21,630
So how does one box find the right plane?

76
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:25,021
Then they pass through tunnels of cameras

77
00:03:25,021 --> 00:03:27,164
that read the label from every side.

78
00:03:28,064 --> 00:03:29,936
Nobody opens your box.

79
00:03:30,236 --> 00:03:32,108
Nobody even looks at it.

80
00:03:33,258 --> 00:03:35,517
The system reads the address, picks

81
00:03:35,517 --> 00:03:37,712
the right plane, and starts moving

82
00:03:37,712 --> 00:03:38,874
the box toward it.

83
00:03:39,774 --> 00:03:41,822
There are more than a hundred and fifty

84
00:03:41,822 --> 00:03:43,974
miles of conveyor belts in this building.

85
00:03:44,924 --> 00:03:47,396
ZERO decides to follow his box.

86
00:03:49,624 --> 00:03:51,784
ZERO does not follow his box.

87
00:03:55,734 --> 00:03:57,798
But the system never loses it.

88
00:03:58,698 --> 00:04:00,666
Every scan updates where it is,

89
00:04:00,966 --> 00:04:03,246
even when ZERO has no idea.

90
00:04:04,046 --> 00:04:06,646
From the moment a box comes off one plane

91
00:04:06,646 --> 00:04:09,182
to the moment it's loaded onto the next,

92
00:04:09,482 --> 00:04:11,507
the whole trip through this building

93
00:04:11,507 --> 00:04:12,914
takes well under an hour.

94
00:04:15,864 --> 00:04:17,760
And it isn't only machines.

95
00:04:18,660 --> 00:04:21,035
Around twenty thousand people work

96
00:04:21,035 --> 00:04:22,572
for UPS in Louisville,

97
00:04:22,872 --> 00:04:24,458
and the night is when this

98
00:04:24,458 --> 00:04:25,800
place needs them most.

99
00:04:26,850 --> 00:04:28,722
Some of them are students.

100
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:32,453
Through a program called Metropolitan

101
00:04:32,453 --> 00:04:35,011
College, people working the overnight Next

102
00:04:35,011 --> 00:04:37,568
Day Air shift can have their tuition paid.

103
00:04:38,468 --> 00:04:40,475
They study by day and sort the

104
00:04:40,475 --> 00:04:41,612
country by night.

105
00:04:42,712 --> 00:04:45,064
Then everything happens in reverse.

106
00:04:45,964 --> 00:04:47,993
Boxes slide down chutes into

107
00:04:47,993 --> 00:04:49,732
containers for one city.

108
00:04:50,632 --> 00:04:52,504
Each container is weighed,

109
00:04:52,804 --> 00:04:54,748
so the plane stays balanced,

110
00:04:54,898 --> 00:04:56,770
and locked into place.

111
00:04:58,670 --> 00:05:01,382
So why fly everything to Kentucky first?

112
00:05:01,682 --> 00:05:03,347
Wouldn't it be faster to fly

113
00:05:03,347 --> 00:05:04,298
straight to mom?

114
00:05:05,198 --> 00:05:07,070
Picture fifty cities.

115
00:05:07,370 --> 00:05:09,924
To connect every city directly to every

116
00:05:09,924 --> 00:05:12,348
other city, you need one thousand two

117
00:05:12,348 --> 00:05:14,378
hundred and twenty-five routes.

118
00:05:14,678 --> 00:05:16,892
Send everything through one hub,

119
00:05:16,892 --> 00:05:18,206
and you need fifty.

120
00:05:19,456 --> 00:05:21,760
Fewer routes means fuller planes.

121
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:23,704
The plane to mom's city

122
00:05:23,704 --> 00:05:25,276
doesn't carry one box.

123
00:05:26,176 --> 00:05:28,954
It carries every box, from every city,

124
00:05:28,954 --> 00:05:30,928
that's going there tonight.

125
00:05:32,028 --> 00:05:33,900
And who pays for all of it?

126
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:36,072
ZERO did.

127
00:05:36,472 --> 00:05:37,384
A little.

128
00:05:38,284 --> 00:05:40,080
The faster the promise, the

129
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:41,476
more the sender pays.

130
00:05:42,376 --> 00:05:44,997
His few dollars rent a tiny piece of a

131
00:05:44,997 --> 00:05:47,618
very large plane, filled with everyone

132
00:05:47,618 --> 00:05:48,928
else's few dollars.

133
00:05:49,228 --> 00:05:50,572
That's the business.

134
00:05:50,972 --> 00:05:53,766
Thousands of small payments, filling big

135
00:05:53,766 --> 00:05:55,652
planes, every single night.

136
00:05:57,102 --> 00:05:59,284
That's also why a company would pour

137
00:05:59,284 --> 00:06:00,678
concrete on this scale.

138
00:06:00,978 --> 00:06:03,956
The expansion that UPS finished here in

139
00:06:03,956 --> 00:06:06,858
2002 cost more than a billion dollars.

140
00:06:07,158 --> 00:06:09,088
At the time, it was the biggest

141
00:06:09,088 --> 00:06:11,142
project in the company's history.

142
00:06:11,442 --> 00:06:14,346
A billion dollars, to save a few hours.

143
00:06:14,746 --> 00:06:16,407
Because those few hours are

144
00:06:16,407 --> 00:06:18,130
exactly what people pay for.

145
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:21,052
And it all happens at night

146
00:06:21,052 --> 00:06:22,216
for a simple reason.

147
00:06:22,516 --> 00:06:24,532
Boxes are picked up in the evening,

148
00:06:24,832 --> 00:06:26,944
sorted while the country sleeps,

149
00:06:27,094 --> 00:06:28,966
and delivered in the morning.

150
00:06:29,116 --> 00:06:30,988
The night is the product.

151
00:06:33,688 --> 00:06:35,680
But tonight, there's a problem.

152
00:06:36,488 --> 00:06:37,736
Remember the rain?

153
00:06:38,838 --> 00:06:40,710
ZERO's label got wet.

154
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:43,336
And the cameras can't read it.

155
00:06:45,010 --> 00:06:46,930
The box is pulled out of the flow.

156
00:06:47,230 --> 00:06:49,102
Now what?

157
00:06:50,252 --> 00:06:52,138
Now, a photo of the label

158
00:06:52,138 --> 00:06:53,420
goes to a person.

159
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:54,800
A real one.

160
00:06:55,100 --> 00:06:57,474
When UPS showed how this works, that

161
00:06:57,474 --> 00:06:59,518
person had about ten seconds to

162
00:06:59,518 --> 00:07:01,100
figure out the ZIP code.

163
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:03,366
If even that fails, someone

164
00:07:03,366 --> 00:07:04,956
checks the box by hand.

165
00:07:05,806 --> 00:07:07,678
Three seconds left.

166
00:07:07,978 --> 00:07:08,794
Got it.

167
00:07:10,094 --> 00:07:12,734
And sometimes the problem isn't one label.

168
00:07:13,634 --> 00:07:16,652
On the night of April second, 2025, with

169
00:07:16,652 --> 00:07:19,595
severe storms on the way, UPS suspended

170
00:07:19,595 --> 00:07:22,537
its Next Day Air sort in Louisville and

171
00:07:22,537 --> 00:07:25,178
canceled incoming domestic flights.

172
00:07:25,478 --> 00:07:27,767
When the center of the network stops,

173
00:07:27,767 --> 00:07:29,438
the whole network feels it.

174
00:07:30,838 --> 00:07:32,998
Tonight, the storm passes.

175
00:07:34,188 --> 00:07:36,252
ZERO's box makes its plane.

176
00:07:38,438 --> 00:07:40,310
And this isn't one building.

177
00:07:42,188 --> 00:07:44,410
In Memphis, FedEx's hub can handle

178
00:07:44,410 --> 00:07:46,633
about four hundred and eighty-four

179
00:07:46,633 --> 00:07:48,332
thousand packages an hour.

180
00:07:48,632 --> 00:07:51,083
In Leipzig, Germany, DHL's hub sorts

181
00:07:51,083 --> 00:07:53,058
more than a hundred and fifty

182
00:07:53,058 --> 00:07:54,896
thousand shipments an hour.

183
00:07:55,996 --> 00:07:57,676
And this is a normal night.

184
00:07:57,976 --> 00:08:00,657
In December, the number of packages on a

185
00:08:00,657 --> 00:08:03,003
single day at Worldport can roughly

186
00:08:03,003 --> 00:08:03,472
double.

187
00:08:04,472 --> 00:08:07,232
Every night, these networks breathe in.

188
00:08:07,532 --> 00:08:09,692
Every morning, they breathe out.

189
00:08:11,292 --> 00:08:13,164
A different plane.

190
00:08:13,464 --> 00:08:15,336
A different truck.

191
00:08:16,436 --> 00:08:18,781
And a van whose route was planned by

192
00:08:18,781 --> 00:08:21,452
software that famously avoids left turns.

193
00:08:21,752 --> 00:08:24,512
Turning left means waiting across traffic.

194
00:08:24,812 --> 00:08:27,647
UPS says skipping those turns saves up to

195
00:08:27,647 --> 00:08:30,068
ten million gallons of fuel a year.

196
00:08:31,668 --> 00:08:33,132
Ten fourteen AM.

197
00:08:33,432 --> 00:08:34,392
Delivered.

198
00:08:34,892 --> 00:08:36,116
Before the party.

199
00:08:38,016 --> 00:08:39,888
ZERO made it too.

200
00:08:40,188 --> 00:08:42,060
He's still not sure how.

201
00:08:42,460 --> 00:08:43,396
You are.

202
00:08:45,446 --> 00:08:46,955
So how did a box cross a

203
00:08:46,955 --> 00:08:48,086
country overnight?

204
00:08:48,386 --> 00:08:50,522
A label that turns it into data.

205
00:08:50,922 --> 00:08:52,530
A trail of scans.

206
00:08:52,730 --> 00:08:54,183
One giant building in the

207
00:08:54,183 --> 00:08:55,346
middle of the night.

208
00:08:55,546 --> 00:08:57,441
A lot of people awake so the rest

209
00:08:57,441 --> 00:08:58,762
of us don't have to be.

210
00:08:58,962 --> 00:09:01,272
And when something went wrong, one

211
00:09:01,272 --> 00:09:03,378
person, with about ten seconds.

212
00:09:09,378 --> 00:09:11,250
And if she doesn't like the gift?

213
00:09:11,550 --> 00:09:13,377
Sending it back is an even

214
00:09:13,377 --> 00:09:14,502
stranger system.
